<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!-- ============================================================
         TURBOLAND THEME - ACCESSIBLE MODE OVERRIDES

         WHAT THIS MODE IS. A legibility variant, not a layout variant.
         Its contract is: every text pair the theme actually renders
         reaches WCAG AA (4.5:1), no meaning is carried by color
         alone, and focus is unmistakable.

         WHAT IT IS NOT. It does not resize anything. Larger controls
         and larger fonts come from the *scale axis*, which is
         orthogonal to mode; TurbolandTheme.ResolveScaleFactor already
         floors this mode at 2x. Making the mode resize things as well
         would produce non-integer cells, which is the one thing the
         character grid exists to prevent.

         Same token names as the authentic palette, and no new colors:
         only re-mappings within the 16-color palette, plus flags that
         switch a cue from hue to chrome.

         THE PALETTE IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, and it is tighter than
         it looks. Of the 16 colors, those reaching AA are:
            on Light Gray (7): Black 9.04, Blue 5.72  - and nothing else
            on Green (2):      Black 6.75             - and nothing else
            on Cyan (3):       Black 7.33, Blue 4.64
         Every override below is forced by that table rather than
         chosen. Where the table offers nothing, the deviation is
         documented rather than papered over.

         ContrastTest asserts all of this against the live resolved
         theme, so these numbers cannot rot.
         ============================================================ -->

    <!-- ============ SELECTION: green to blue ============
         White on green is 3.11:1 and fails; white on blue is 13.29:1.
         Blue/white is the only high-contrast selection pair the palette
         offers.

         The menu override is included for consistency rather than
         necessity: black on green already measures 6.75:1 and passes.
         Overriding it too is what makes every selection in the theme a
         single color. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.MenuSelectionBackground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.1}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.MenuSelectionForeground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.15}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.FieldSelectionBackground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.1}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.ControlSelected" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.1}" />

    <!-- ============ ACCELERATORS: color to underline ============
         Authentic mode marks the hot key by hue alone: red on the menu,
         yellow on buttons and fields. All three fail AA (3.34, 2.92,
         2.69) and also fail WCAG 1.4.1, since hue is the only cue.

         No better hue exists. The character must stay legible both on
         the resting surface and on the selection bar, and no single
         palette color does both. So the hot key gives up having a
         color of its own and inherits the label's, which makes it
         exactly as legible as the text it sits in, in every state, by
         construction rather than by another lookup table.

         This is a flag rather than four x:Null brush overrides,
         because x:Null cannot express an override: WPF cannot tell a
         null dictionary entry from a missing one, so the lookup falls
         through to the authentic color and the override silently does
         nothing.

         The underline flag below is what carries the signal instead. -->
    <sys:Boolean x:Key="Turboland.Flag.AcceleratorInheritColor">True</sys:Boolean>

    <!-- The status-bar key is not an AcceleratorText (StatusBarHint draws it as a
         fixed Run), so the flag above does not reach it and it needs its own
         override. Red on light gray is 3.34:1. Black is 9.04:1 and is the only
         choice: the palette offers exactly two colors reaching AA on light gray,
         and the other one is DisabledForeground, so a status-bar key painted in it
         would read as disabled. The red anchor is lost, but a key such as "F1"
         remains identifiable by position and content, so no information is left
         carried by color alone. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.StatusBarAccelerator" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.0}" />

    <!-- Color alone fails WCAG 1.4.1, and the accelerator now has no hue of its
         own at all, so the underline is what identifies the access key. -->
    <sys:Boolean x:Key="Turboland.Flag.AcceleratorUnderline">True</sys:Boolean>

    <!-- ============ FOCUS ============
         A thicker ring is affordable only because focus chrome is drawn
         *outside* the layout box, so thickening it costs no layout at
         all; a ring inside the box would resize every focused control.
         The offset must stay equal and opposite to the thickness or the
         ring eats into the content. GridAlignmentTest pins that. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="Turboland.Metric.FocusBorder">2</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="Turboland.Size.FocusBorder">2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Turboland.Size.FocusRingOffset">-2</Thickness>
    <!-- The ring overhangs by 2 here rather than 1, so a clipping container has to
         reserve 2 on the left and top or this mode loses exactly the focus indicator
         it exists to strengthen. Right/bottom is still governed by the button
         shadow, which does not change per mode. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="Turboland.Size.ChromeInset">2,2,9,7</Thickness>

    <!-- Yellow is the strongest ring signal available against the green and gray
         faces, and it is never used as a face color, so a ring can never blend
         into whatever it surrounds. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.FocusOutline" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.14}" />

    <!-- White on the cyan field measures 2.86:1; black is 7.33:1. Recoloring to
         black removes the hue-based focus cue, which is why the 2px ring above is
         part of the same change: the signal moves from hue to chrome. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.FieldFocusedForeground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.0}" />

    <!-- ============ DISABLED ============
         Dark gray on light gray is 3.21:1. Blue is 5.72:1, keeps a clear
         "not enabled" signal, and cannot be confused with the black used
         for enabled text. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.DisabledForeground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.1}" />

    <!-- A disabled push button cannot be fixed by dimming its label: black is the
         only color reaching AA on green, and black is already the enabled label.
         So the *face* changes instead, to light gray. That reads as the universal
         "greyed out" and keeps a disabled button obviously distinct from an enabled
         green one; blue on light gray is then 5.72:1. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.ButtonDisabledFace" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.7}" />

    <!-- ============ EDITOR ============
         Dark gray on black is 2.82:1; light gray is 9.04:1. Overridden even though
         no control currently renders the gutter, so the pair is correct as soon as
         one does. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turboland.Brush.EditorGutterForeground" Color="{DynamicResource Turboland.Color.Palette.7}" />

</ResourceDictionary>
